  <section class="brief">
    <h2>A name that wraps around the bottle.</h2>
    <p>The brief was to create a gin label for Publicis Machine. Starting with the name, the design explored oversized typography, simple composition and the transparent surface of the bottle.</p>
    <p>MACHINE GIN is arranged as a compact typographic block. Large, stacked letters establish the rhythm, while smaller product details create scale and contrast.</p>
  </section>
  <figure class="court-crop"><img src="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/figma-tennis-court.jpg" alt="Tennis court art direction study" loading="lazy" width="1904" height="2544" /></figure>

  <section class="project-copy" aria-label="Physical Testing"><h2 class="eyebrow">The physical experiment</h2>
    <p>Early labels were printed, cut and wrapped around an existing bottle. Testing the artwork in three dimensions revealed a different way to read the name: through the glass, from the opposite side.</p>
  </section>
  <figure class="type-study"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/figma-type-study.png" width="2500" height="1960" alt="MACHINE GIN lettering study" /></figure>
  <figure class="pink-bottle"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/figma-pink-bottle.jpg" width="1328" height="912" alt="Machine Gin bottle on a pink tennis court" /></figure>
  <section class="project-copy"><h2 class="eyebrow">The wordmark</h2>
    <p>MACHINE GIN is set as a compact typographic block. The breaks within the name create the rhythm of the label, while smaller product text gives the oversized lettering a contrasting scale.</p>
  </section>
  <figure class="tennis-motion"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/figma-tennis-motion.jpg" width="2944" height="1648" alt="Tennis player captured in motion" /></figure>
  <section class="context">
    <p class="eyebrow">Machine Gin / In context</p>
    <h2>Machine <em>in context.</em></h2>
    <p>Beyond the bottle, a playful photographic world of pink tennis courts, motion blur and afternoon cocktails.</p>
    <p class="eyebrow">Packaging concept / circa 2022</p>
  </section>
  <section class="process" aria-label="Construction">
    <p class="eyebrow">Publicis Machine / The process</p>
    <div class="project-copy"><h2>Designed backwards.<br />Read forwards.</h2>
      <p>The defining idea emerged through physical experimentation. I printed variations, wrapped them around a bottle and examined the lettering from different angles. Reversing the artwork made the letters readable through the glass. The material became part of the message.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="studies">
    <figure><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/parity/1277-14558-1be36.png" alt="Detail of the stacked label lettering" /></figure>
    <figure><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/parity/1277-14558-e037c.png" alt="Four variations on GIN" /><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/parity/1277-14558-1be36.png" alt="" /></figure>
  </div>
  <section class="machine-art-direction">
    <div><h2>Machine Gin / Art Direction</h2><p>From typographic packaging to a playful visual world of tennis, summer light and cocktails.</p></div>
    <p class="eyebrow">01 &nbsp;&nbsp; Packaging · Typography · Art Direction</p>
    <blockquote>A study in perception: the bottle, the label and the way we see through both.</blockquote>
    <div class="machine-media-slot" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="machine-video machine-video-rotate">
      <video src="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/construction-film.mp4" poster="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/construction-poster.jpg" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="Rotating Machine Gin bottle study"></video>
      <img src="/assets/projects/publicis-machine/construction-poster.jpg" alt="Machine Gin bottle study" width="800" height="643" />
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="process"><p class="eyebrow">Two directions</p><h2>One name. Two expressions.</h2></section>
  <section class="project-copy"><h2 class="eyebrow">Two expressions</h2>
    <p>Two expressions emerge from the same typographic idea: a restrained, monochrome approach and a vivid botanical exploration using colour, transparency and oversized lettering.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="project-copy"><h2 class="eyebrow">Concept studies</h2>
    <p>The black-gin experiment, bottle renders and tennis imagery extend the original packaging study into a broader visual direction. These are exploratory concepts, not a produced campaign.</p>
  </section>
</div>

